<h2>The application</h2>
<p>The petitioner sought a further eight-week extension of the mandate of an arbitral tribunal comprising three former judges of the superior courts. The arbitration itself was at the very last stage: by a procedural order of 28 May 2026, the matter had been reserved for pronouncement of the award. In October 2026, a communication from the presiding arbitrator&rsquo;s office indicated that the award required more time. Rather than let the mandate run out with the award unpronounced, the petitioner moved the court; the respondent&rsquo;s counsel accepted notice and stated there was no objection.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the order (para 5):</strong> &ldquo;the mandate of the learned Arbitral Tribunal is extended for a further period of 8 weeks from today&rdquo; — with any intervening period also regularised.</p>
</div>

<h2>Section 29A in brief</h2>
<p>Section 29A disciplines domestic arbitration with a statutory clock: twelve months from completion of pleadings, a further six by consent, and thereafter only court-sanctioned time. The provision was designed against the old culture of arbitrations that meandered for years; its cost is that even a well-run arbitration needs a court visit if the award cannot be delivered within the consented period. The courts&rsquo; working approach distinguishes between extensions sought to continue dragging proceedings and extensions sought to let a completed proceeding reach its natural end. This case sat firmly in the second category — everything was done except the pronouncement.</p>

<h2>Practical sequence for parties</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Track the clock from pleadings.</strong> Diarise the twelve-month mark and the consent extension; do not discover expiry from the opposite side&rsquo;s objection.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Consent early.</strong> The six-month consent extension needs no court and should be recorded in a procedural order while time remains.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Move the court before expiry where possible.</strong> An application filed in time keeps the mandate alive pending disposal; if filed after, seek regularisation of the gap, as was granted here.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Keep it consensual at the award stage.</strong> Where the award is reserved, opposing an extension rarely serves either side — a lapsed mandate benefits no one and invites satellite litigation.</div>
</div>

<h2>The takeaway</h2>
<p>For commercial parties arbitrating in Delhi, this order is a clean illustration of Section 29A working as intended: a short, consent-based extension, granted in a two-page order, protecting months of completed hearings from a technical lapse. The discipline of the provision remains real — but where the only step left is pronouncement, the courts&rsquo; door is open, and the sensible course for both sides is to walk through it together.</p>
